    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d273">

                <pb facs="0346" n="262" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg262"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 711.6221/165</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Swiss Chargé</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hübscher</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 495</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-08-13">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 13, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my memorandum of June 1,
                    1917, relative to the competence of Swiss consuls, acting in the interests of
                    absent German heirs, to receive personal property in settlement of inheritances,
                    I have the honor now to ask if you will kindly instruct Swiss consular officers
                    in the United States to defer, for the time being, the acceptance of money due
                    German subjects from estates in this country, inasmuch as it is possible that
                    the general subject may be taken up in war legislation before the present
                    Congress.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
